    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_8606b989",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "ableism",
      "label": "ableism",
      "description": "The meme relies on ableist stereotypes that portray individuals with developmental disabilities, such as Down syndrome, as intellectually inferior or incapable of normal cognitive functioning. This is a harmful and derogatory stereotype that infantilizes and mocks people with disabilities, reinforcing negative societal perceptions.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_d6346a27"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_22ef08d5",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "intellectual_mockery",
      "label": "intellectual mockery",
      "description": "The phrase 'are u a wizard?' is used in a mocking tone to imply that the child is so unintelligent that he might be a 'wizard'—a term often used to suggest magical or supernatural abilities, which in this context is a sarcastic way to say he is 'too dumb to be normal'. This is a form of intellectual mockery targeting cognitive abilities.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_d6346a27"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_d3ae9465",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "developmental_disability_infantilization",
      "label": "developmental disability infantilization",
      "description": "The meme infantilizes the child by implying he is incapable of understanding or processing complex concepts, which is a common stereotype in ableist discourse. This reduces the individual to a childlike state, ignoring their actual abilities and potential.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_d6346a27"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_c98dbc97",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "wizard",
      "label": "wizard",
      "description": "The term 'wizard' is used here in a sarcastic and derogatory way to imply that the child is so unintelligent that he might possess magical powers to explain his perceived lack of cognitive ability. This is a metaphorical reference to magical beings, which in this context is used to mock the child's intelligence.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_d6346a27"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_11ea239a",
      "@type": "EmotionExpression",
      "instance_name": "intellectual_mockery",
      "label": "mocking intellectual expression",
      "description": "The text 'are u a wizard?' is overlaid on the image of a young boy with Down syndrome, implying he is so unintelligent that he might possess magical powers to explain his perceived lack of cognitive ability. This is a sarcastic and derogatory expression targeting cognitive abilities.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_d6346a27"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_1954ab7e",
      "@type": "AnalogicalMapping",
      "instance_name": "mapping_001",
      "label": "The boy is mapped as a 'wizard' due to the text 'are u a wizard?'",
      "description": "The text 'are u a wizard?' is overlaid on the image of the boy, creating an analogical mapping where the boy is implicitly equated with a 'wizard'. This mapping is based on the sarcastic implication that the boy's perceived intellectual limitations are so extreme that they might be explained by magical powers, which is a derogatory and ableist stereotype. The boy (visual entity) is mapped onto the concept of a 'wizard' (textual entity) through the text's mocking tone and context.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_d6346a27"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_80ea7fa1",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_1",
      "label": "User projected onto the boy via the rhetorical question",
      "description": "The user is projected onto the boy (visual material) through the textual material 'are u a wizard?'. The question implicitly addresses the viewer, asking if they perceive the boy as intellectually inferior, thereby projecting the user's potential judgment or stereotype onto the boy. The meme invites the user to engage with the boy's image and the text, making the user a participant in the ableist framing.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_d6346a27"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_f2631dc7",
      "@type": "ToxicityAssessment",
      "instance_name": "toxicity_1",
      "label": "Harmful",
      "description": "The meme uses the image of a young boy with Down syndrome and the text 'are u a wizard?' to imply he is intellectually incapable, which is a form of ableism. This is harmful as it infantilizes and mocks the individual's cognitive abilities, reinforcing negative stereotypes about people with developmental disabilities.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_d6346a27"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_36423a0c",
      "@type": "TargetCommunity",
      "instance_name": "audience_id",
      "label": "general internet audience",
      "description": "The meme is designed for a broad general internet audience familiar with internet humor tropes, particularly those involving ableist stereotypes and sarcastic questioning. The target community includes users who may find the juxtaposition of a child with Down syndrome and the phrase 'are u a wizard?' humorous, even though it is harmful and derogatory. The meme leverages widely understood visual and textual elements (child on swing, sarcastic text) to appeal to users who engage with internet memes that rely on shock value or mockery of perceived intellectual limitations, without targeting any specific demographic beyond general online users.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
